# ThreadSanitizer suppressions for the display-lock threading gate
# (build/tests/check under -fsanitize=thread).
#
# libdbus, pulled in transitively by SDL for desktop integration, locks its
# own internal mutexes in opposite orders across the connection paths reached
# from XOpenDisplay and XCloseDisplay. TSan intermittently reports this as a
# lock-order inversion between two libdbus mutexes (M0 => M1 => M0). The cycle
# is entirely inside libdbus, a third-party library we do not control, and both
# mutexes are libdbus-internal; our own recursive display lock is a separate
# mutex in src/display.c and is unaffected.
#
# called_from_lib disables interception only when libdbus is the direct caller
# of the mutex op, which is exactly the observed cycle (pthread_mutex_lock is
# called straight from libdbus frames). This is deliberately narrow: a genuine
# inversion involving our own display lock is locked from src/display.c, not
# from libdbus, so it stays fully instrumented. A module-wide deadlock:libdbus-1
# rule would instead swallow any report that merely transits libdbus, including
# a real cross-boundary cycle against our mutex, so it is intentionally omitted.
called_from_lib:libdbus-1.so
